PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
Current answer remains **1999**, now with near-consensus. The decisive hinge is still the 1980 lunar conversion. Prevailing evidence: the lunar month-8 new moon in 1980 was **1980-09-10 06:46 Beijing time**, so lunar 8/1 = Sep 10, Mid-Autumn 8/15 = **1980-09-24**, and 1980-10-01 = lunar **8/22**. Thus the person’s lunar birthday is 八月廿二, and the target is the next year after 1980 when lunar 8/22 falls on Gregorian 10-01, equivalently when Mid-Autumn 8/15 falls on **09-24**. In **1999**, Mid-Autumn was **1999-09-24**, so lunar 8/22 was **1999-10-01**. No year 1981–1998 appears to match.

Current round: C1–C5, C7–C12 all support **1999**. C6 alone switched to **2067**, claiming the 1980 month-8 new moon was **1980-09-09 18:02 Beijing time**, which would make 1980-10-01 lunar 8/23 and require Mid-Autumn on 09-23. Under that premise, 1999 would give lunar 8/23 on Oct 2, while 2067 Mid-Autumn Sep 23 would give lunar 8/23 on Oct 1. This is now the only serious counterargument. It directly conflicts with the previously cited 1980 new-moon time and with the common 1980 Mid-Autumn date of Sep 24.

Earlier 2056, 2078, 2066, 2059, 1991 claims depended on the disputed 8/23 conversion or other table errors; most have been abandoned. Even if 2056 could match under some convention, it is later than 1999 and cannot be the “next” if 1999 already satisfies the corrected condition.

Unresolved: an authoritative Beijing-time PRC/HKO calendar or precise astronomical new-moon time for 1980 could settle C6’s 09-09 18:02 claim vs the 09-10 06:46 claim. If C6 were correct, the answer could become 2067; under the prevailing evidence and majority position, my persistent stance remains **1999**.
}

